.\" Man page for krb, the Kirby command line tool (src/main.c).
.TH KRB 1 "October 3, 2026" "Kirby" "Kirby Manual"
.SH NAME
krb \- run, build and explore programs written in Kirby
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B krb
.RB [ \-h ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.br
.B krb
.I command
.RI [ args ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B krb
is the command line tool of the Kirby language, a small scripting language
with a compiler and a virtual machine.
It runs Kirby source files, starts an interactive session, builds a program
into a single executable, and shows how a file is tokenized and parsed.
.PP
The first argument is the command, unless it starts with
.BR \- .
Everything after the command belongs to the command, or to the script that it
runs.
A script's own arguments are never read as options of
.BR krb .
.SH OPTIONS
Options are only read when no command is given.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the version and a usage summary to standard error, and exit with status
0.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the Kirby version to standard output, and exit with status 0.
.PP
With no command and no option, or with a command that does not exist,
.B krb
prints the usage summary to standard error and exits with status 64.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\fBinit\fR
Create
.B kirby.project.toml
in the current directory, with its settings commented out.
If the file already exists, print an error and leave it as it is.
.TP
\fBrun\fR [\fIpath\fR] [\fIargs\fR...]
Compile and run the Kirby file at
.IR path .
.I args
are the script's own arguments.
Without a path, or with
.B \-\-
in place of one, run the file named by
.B bin
in
.BR kirby.project.toml .
For example,
.B krb run \-\- one two
gives
.B one two
to that file.
If the project has no bin file, print a message and exit with status 1.
.TP
\fBexample\fR \fIname\fR [\fIargs\fR...]
Run
.IR name .krb
from the project's examples folder.
.I args
are the script's own arguments.
.TP
\fBconfig\fR [\fIkey\fR]
Print the project settings from
.B kirby.project.toml
in the current directory.
With no key, print the file.
With
.B bin
or
.BR examples ,
print the value of that setting.
If there is no
.BR kirby.project.toml ,
say so and suggest
.BR "krb init" .
.TP
\fBrepl\fR
Start an interactive session.
Each line that is typed is evaluated as it is entered.
Type
.B help
to print the address of the documentation, and
.B exit
or press Ctrl\-D to quit.
The REPL takes no arguments.
.TP
\fBexec\fR \fIsource\fR [\fIargs\fR...]
Run the Kirby source code given as the string
.IR source .
.I args
are the script's own arguments.
.TP
\fBcompile\fR \fIpath\fR
Compile the file at
.I path
without running it.
On success print
.B Compiled!\&
to standard error.
A compile error is reported and ends with status 65.
.TP
\fBbuild\fR \fIpath\fR \fB\-o\fR \fIoutput\fR [\fB\-\-runtime\fR \fIruntime\fR]
Build the file at
.I path
into a single executable at
.I output
that runs it.
The executable is a copy of the runtime, which has the virtual machine but no
compiler, with the compiled program attached to it.
It needs neither
.B krb
nor the source file, and it receives its arguments the way
.B krb run
passes them.
.IP
The runtime is
.B krb\-runtime
in the same directory as
.BR krb ,
unless
.B \-\-runtime
names a different one.
It must be the same version of Kirby as
.BR krb ,
or the executable refuses to run.
An
.I output
that already exists is replaced.
.B build
is supported on Linux and macOS.
.TP
\fBlex\fR \fIpath\fR
Print the tokens of the file at
.IR path ,
one per line.
.TP
\fBparse\fR \fIpath\fR
Print the syntax tree of the file at
.I path
as an expression, such as
.BR "(call @println (+ 1 2))" .
.SH SCRIPT ARGUMENTS
A script reads its arguments with
.B @argc()
and
.BR @argv(\fIindex\fR) .
.PP
.B @argv(0)
is the program: the path given to
.BR "krb run" ,
the path of the file that
.B krb example
runs,
.B exec
for
.BR "krb exec" ,
.B repl
in the REPL, and the path that a built executable was started with.
.B @argv(1)
onward are the script's own arguments, and an index past the last one gives
.BR nil .
.B @argc()
counts the program and the arguments, so
.B krb run file.krb a b
and a built
.B ./app a b
both see 3.
.SH PROJECT FILE
.B krb run
without a path,
.B krb example
and
.B krb config
read
.B kirby.project.toml
from the current directory.
It is a TOML file with two settings, both strings:
.TP
.B bin
The file that
.B krb run
runs when it is given no path, such as
.BR bin/main.krb .
.TP
.B examples
The folder that
.B krb example
looks in.
The default is
.BR examples .
.PP
.B krb init
creates the file with both settings commented out.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B kirby.project.toml
The settings of a project, read from the current directory.
.TP
.B krb\-runtime
The runtime that
.B krb build
copies.
It is installed next to
.BR krb .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, or the REPL was left.
.TP
.B 1
.B krb run
was given no path and the project has no bin file.
.TP
.B 64
The command line was wrong: no command, a command that does not exist, or
arguments that the command does not accept.
The usage summary is printed to standard error.
.TP
.B 65
A compile error in the program.
.TP
.B 70
A runtime error in the program.
.TP
.B 71
An error from the operating system, such as a file that cannot be opened or
written.
.PP
A script can end with its own status by calling
.BR @exit() .
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
krb run path/to/file.krb          # run a file
krb run                           # run the project's bin file
krb run \-\- arg1 arg2              # give arguments to the project's bin file
krb example hello                 # run examples/hello.krb
krb exec \(aq@println("Hello World");\(aq
krb build path/to/file.krb \-o app # make ./app, which runs the file
krb parse path/to/file.krb
krb init                          # start a project in this folder
krb config bin                    # print the bin setting
krb repl
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
The Kirby documentation: https://github.com/kirbylang/kirbylang#documentation
